## The three principles

1. **Single source of truth** — one store for the whole app
2. **State is read-only** — the only way to change it is dispatching an action
3. **Changes come from pure functions** — reducers take `(state, action)` and return new state

---

## The flow

```text
UI → dispatch(action) → reducer(state, action) → new state → UI re-renders
```

Unidirectional and traceable. Every change has a named action, which is what makes time-travel debugging possible.

---

## Redux Toolkit — the modern form

```jsx
import { createSlice, configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const cartSlice = createSlice({
  name: 'cart',
  initialState: { items: [] },
  reducers: {
    // Looks like mutation — Immer makes it immutable underneath
    addItem(state, action) {
      state.items.push(action.payload);
    },
    removeItem(state, action) {
      state.items = state.items.filter((i) => i.id !== action.payload);
    },
  },
});

export const { addItem, removeItem } = cartSlice.actions;

export const store = configureStore({
  reducer: { cart: cartSlice.reducer },
});
```

```jsx
function Cart() {
  const items = useSelector((s) => s.cart.items);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  return <button onClick={() => dispatch(addItem(product))}>Add</button>;
}
```

Action types, action creators and immutable update logic all come from `createSlice`. The old three-files-per-feature ceremony is gone.

---

## Why the reputation for boilerplate

Original Redux needed hand-written action type constants, action creators, switch-statement reducers with manual spreading, and `redux-thunk` wired up by hand. Redux Toolkit collapses all of that, and configures the DevTools and thunk middleware by default.

> If asked "is Redux still relevant?" — the accurate answer is that **Redux Toolkit** is, particularly on large teams where enforced structure and excellent DevTools are worth the ceremony. Its share has shrunk mainly because React Query took over server state, which was most of what people stored in Redux.

---

## Selectors

```jsx
// ❌ New array every call → re-renders on every store change
const visible = useSelector((s) => s.todos.filter((t) => !t.done));

// ✅ Memoised with createSelector
const selectVisible = createSelector(
  [(s) => s.todos],
  (todos) => todos.filter((t) => !t.done)
);
const visible = useSelector(selectVisible);
```

`useSelector` compares by reference, so a selector returning a fresh array defeats the check. This is the most frequent Redux performance bug.

## Why bother

Without it, clicking "like" means: spinner → 300ms → the heart fills. The app feels sluggish even though the network is fine. With it, the heart fills instantly and reverts in the rare failure case.

---

## React 19's useOptimistic

```jsx
import { useOptimistic } from 'react';

function LikeButton({ post }) {
  const [optimisticLikes, addOptimisticLike] = useOptimistic(
    post.likes,
    (current, increment) => current + increment
  );

  async function handleLike() {
    addOptimisticLike(1);          // UI updates immediately
    await likePost(post.id);       // if this throws, React reverts automatically
  }

  return <button onClick={handleLike}>♥ {optimisticLikes}</button>;
}
```

React discards the optimistic value once the real state arrives — or when the action fails — so there is no manual rollback.

---

## React Query's version

```jsx
useMutation({
  mutationFn: toggleLike,

  async onMutate(newValue) {
    await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['post', id] });   // stop in-flight refetches
    const previous = queryClient.getQueryData(['post', id]);        // snapshot

    queryClient.setQueryData(['post', id], (old) => ({
      ...old,
      likes: old.likes + 1,
    }));

    return { previous };            // passed to onError as context
  },

  onError(err, newValue, context) {
    queryClient.setQueryData(['post', id], context.previous);   // roll back
  },

  onSettled() {
    queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['post', id] });   // resync either way
  },
});
```

The `cancelQueries` call is the detail people miss — without it, a refetch that was already in flight can land after your optimistic write and overwrite it.

---

## When to use it

| Good fit | Bad fit |
|----------|---------|
| Like, favourite, bookmark | Payments |
| Toggling a checkbox | Anything with server-side validation you cannot predict |
| Reordering a list | Operations with side effects the user would notice reversing |
| Adding a comment | Irreversible actions |

> The test: **could you predict the server's response, and is being wrong occasionally acceptable?** If either answer is no, show a spinner instead.

---

## Communicating failure

A silent rollback is confusing — the user sees their action undo itself for no apparent reason. Pair it with a toast: *"Could not save. Try again."* Optimistic UI without failure feedback is worse than no optimistic UI.

## The whole store

```jsx
import { create } from 'zustand';

const useCart = create((set, get) => ({
  items: [],
  add: (item) => set((s) => ({ items: [...s.items, item] })),
  remove: (id) => set((s) => ({ items: s.items.filter((i) => i.id !== id) })),
  total: () => get().items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0),
}));
```

No provider, no reducer file, no action type constants.

```jsx
function Badge() {
  // Subscribes to this slice only — never re-renders when other fields change
  const count = useCart((s) => s.items.length);
  return <span>{count}</span>;
}
```

---

## Comparison

| | Redux Toolkit | Zustand |
|---|---------------|---------|
| Provider required | Yes | No |
| Bundle size | ~11KB | ~1KB |
| Selectors | Via `useSelector` | Built in |
| Action log / time travel | Excellent | Basic, via middleware |
| Enforced structure | Yes | No — you impose it |
| Boilerplate | Moderate | Minimal |
| Async | Thunks / RTK Query | Just write `async` in the action |

---

## Async needs no middleware

```jsx
const useUsers = create((set) => ({
  users: [],
  loading: false,
  fetch: async () => {
    set({ loading: true });
    const users = await api.getUsers();
    set({ users, loading: false });
  },
}));
```

Redux needs thunks or sagas for this. Zustand actions are plain functions, so `await` works directly.

---

## The selector gotcha

```jsx
// ❌ New object every render → re-renders on every store change
const { items, total } = useCart((s) => ({ items: s.items, total: s.total }));

// ✅ Separate subscriptions
const items = useCart((s) => s.items);
const total = useCart((s) => s.total);

// ✅ Or supply an equality function
import { useShallow } from 'zustand/react/shallow';
const { items, total } = useCart(useShallow((s) => ({ items: s.items, total: s.total })));
```

Same underlying issue as `useSelector` in Redux: the default comparison is by reference.

---

## Choosing between them

**Redux Toolkit** when the team is large and benefits from enforced conventions, when you want a complete audit trail of state changes, or when RTK Query fits your data layer.

**Zustand** when you want a store without ceremony and trust the team to keep it organised. It is the common default for new projects that need more than Context.

> Both are correct answers. What matters is explaining the trade-off — structure versus simplicity — rather than declaring a winner.

## The hook

```jsx
function usePersistentState(key, initialValue) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(() => {
    try {
      const stored = localStorage.getItem(key);
      return stored ? JSON.parse(stored) : initialValue;
    } catch {
      return initialValue;    // corrupt JSON or storage disabled
    }
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    try {
      localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value));
    } catch {
      // quota exceeded, or Safari private mode
    }
  }, [key, value]);

  return [value, setValue];
}
```

Both `try/catch` blocks matter. Storage throws in private browsing and when the quota is full, and a crash on startup because of one bad key is a bad failure mode.

---

## Storage options

| | Persists | Size | Sync | Sent to server |
|---|----------|------|------|----------------|
| `localStorage` | Until cleared | ~5MB | Yes (blocking) | No |
| `sessionStorage` | Until tab closes | ~5MB | Yes | No |
| `IndexedDB` | Until cleared | Large | No (async) | No |
| Cookies | Configurable | 4KB | Yes | **Yes, every request** |

`localStorage` is synchronous, so writing large objects on every keystroke blocks the main thread. Debounce the write if the value changes frequently.

---

## The SSR problem

```jsx
// ❌ Server renders 'light', client renders 'dark' → hydration mismatch
const [theme] = useState(() => localStorage.getItem('theme') ?? 'light');

// ✅ Match the server on first render, then correct after mount
const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
useEffect(() => {
  const stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
  if (stored) setTheme(stored);
}, []);
```

For themes specifically, the standard fix is a small blocking inline script in `<head>` that sets a class on `<html>` before React runs — that avoids the flash of the wrong theme entirely.

---

## With a store

```jsx
import { persist } from 'zustand/middleware';

const useSettings = create(
  persist(
    (set) => ({ theme: 'light', setTheme: (theme) => set({ theme }) }),
    { name: 'settings' }
  )
);
```

Redux has `redux-persist` for the same job.

---

## Security

**Never store auth tokens in `localStorage`.** Any script on the page — including one from a compromised dependency — can read it. Use an `httpOnly` cookie, which JavaScript cannot access at all.

Also remember storage is per-origin and shared across tabs, so a `storage` event listener is how you keep multiple tabs in sync.
